      ],
      "mergeID": null,
      "parentID": "5a6faa876feffe4d02a0629d",
      "pinned": false,
      "postDeleted": false,
      "postID": "5a5d66760c8f72447a01beef",
      "private": false,
      "reactions": null,
      "sourceLink": null,
      "spam": false,
      "value": "@{5a6c4d4edb821e1569d46de3|full_name}: As an embedded systems engineer and systems architect, I can tell you're statement is based on speculation. Sign language recognition is as easy as matching the change on movement with that of sign language movements. This is a simple common-case usage for artificial neural networks. I'm not disclosing numbers, but with the investments that vrchat has gotten, they can afford developers who can do this development and integration within half a year. Disgarding all that, I do think text chat capabilities would be best as long as the users have the option to toggle visibility on a per-player basis, much like with muting voice chat.",
      "viewerReactions": null
    },
